.TH KEYUPTEST 1
.SH NAME
keyuptest \- regression test for key-release handling in Tk
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keyuptest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Keyuptest
checks that key-release events are not delivered to Tk widgets as
typed text.
It prints one line per check and a final
.B PASS
or
.BR "SOME CHECKS FAILED" .
.PP
A backend sends a release for every press on the same keyboard channel
as the press itself, encoded as
.BI Keyup| "(key & 0x7ff)"
(see
.IR keyboard (6)).
If nothing strips that marker before delivery, every release falls
through to the bare-key binding in
.IR entry (9)
or
.IR text (9)
and is inserted literally, putting a private-use-area codepoint
between every real character \(em the visible symptom being text that
looks correct but is twice the expected length.
.PP
Rather than driving the window system, the test calls
.IR tk (2)'s
.B keyboard
entry point directly with exact key codes and reads the widget back
with
.BR ".e get" ,
so it is deterministic and needs no synthetic input.
It checks four things:
that typing with a matching release per press yields only the typed
characters;
that ordinary presses still insert (so the release filter cannot be
passing by swallowing everything);
that releases alone insert nothing;
and that
.BR German ,
.BR Grave ,
.BR Acute ,
and
.B Circumflex
are still delivered \(em those four numerically satisfy the same mask
as
.RB ( 0xef00
.B &
.BR 0xf800 " == " 0xe800 ),
so a filter written without care swallows real dead-key presses.
.PP
It allocates its own display and so needs a graphical backend; under a
headless
.IR emu (1)
it exits reporting that no display could be allocated.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/keyuptest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tk (2),
.IR keyboard (6)
.SH BUGS
It exercises only the delivery path, not a real keyboard: a backend
that encodes releases differently from
.IR keyboard (6)
would pass here and still misbehave.
